"rpresser" <rpres...@gmail.com> wrote:

> That is an interesting idea indeed. Unfortunately,
> it doesn't seem practical.

Hold that thought.

> The contents of containers not on the current
> level are not easily accessible to the program at
> the time you would need them to be.

Sure they are. Slogging through all the visited
levels might take a bit of time, but it is something
the game mechanics have to do anyway when you
revisit those levels, so the guts of the code
already exist.

> The endgame routine would need to load each level
> you visited to look for containers,

This choice...

> or else keep a linked list of named containers in
> memory at all times so it knows what levels they
> are on.

... not this choice; container contents lists can be
huge memory consumers; gathering all such lists into
memory at once looks like a resource hog idea.

> If the latter, then it needs to revisit that list
> every time you name something, every time a
> gelatinous cube eats a chest, etc.

Those things only occur on the current level anyway,
so keeping the information "by level", as is done
now, seems the simpler approach.

Given all that, though, I'm not too sure I'd sit
through the list of all the rubbish I tend to pack
into containers atop altars, just to keep it out of
the grasp of monsters. There tend to be hundreds of
items in such containers. I think going with a
single container name (I name mine "Ascension Kit"
in a fit of optimism rarely seen on this planet)
and a single container by that name looks to me like
a better plan.

